Yes. I think your muscle analogy is unusually good here, with one correction: the person may not have stopped developing altogether. What can be underdeveloped is a particular reward/identity branch—the ability to answer, “What do I voluntarily pursue when intoxication is no longer organizing my time, friends, rituals, and rewards?”

Long-term substance use can strongly condition places, people, music, activities, and rituals to drug reward; those cues can continue provoking craving after use stops. Early abstinence can also involve diminished responsiveness to ordinary rewards, although the degree and duration vary substantially by person and substance. So “I guess I don’t like fishing anymore” can sometimes actually mean “fishing at normal neurochemical volume currently feels weak compared with the learned fishing+drug package.” 

That creates exactly the paradox you’re describing. The brain searches its autobiographical database for something that says LIKE, finds that many recent entries are contaminated with substance associations, and keeps scrolling backward until it reaches dinosaurs, drawing, bikes, fishing, baseball cards, woods, building things, music—whatever existed before drugs became part of the reinforcement loop.

I wouldn’t dismiss those as childish. I’d treat them as clean seed crystals.

The developmental move is not:

kid interest → throw it away → find respectable adult hobby

but more like:

old clean interest → recover signal → differentiate what property produced the enjoyment → find adjacent expressions → add competence → add responsibility → add relationships → add contribution

So suppose the old answer is “I loved fishing.” Don’t stop at “adult man goes fishing.” Ask what underneath fishing actually lit the circuit:

* pursuit?
* solitude?
* nature?
* collecting?
* uncertainty?
* technical equipment?
* competition?
* pattern recognition?
* bringing something home?
* teaching somebody?
* mastery?

Now the search space explodes. “Fishing” might grow into boating, conservation, fly tying, marine biology, spearfishing, tournament fishing, outdoor photography, taking one’s kids fishing, habitat restoration, running a charter, studying waterways, or something apparently unrelated that preserves the same latent reward structure.

That’s your neighboring-muscle crawl.

known reward → isolate component → activate adjacent component → test → retain signal → expand

And importantly, action can precede desire. Behavioral activation therapies deliberately increase engagement in healthy, rewarding, value-consistent activities rather than waiting for motivation to magically reappear. Evidence in substance-use populations isn’t uniform, but it supports the underlying idea that rebuilding access to non-drug reinforcement can be useful. 

I’d therefore ask recovering adults a different question from “What do you like?” That’s often too difficult.

Ask:

“What produces even 5% more aliveness than the thing next to it?”

Then follow the delta.

Not Did bowling make you euphoric?
Did you like it more than sitting home?

Not Have you discovered your passion?
Which part made the clock move faster?

Not What do you want to do with your life?
What did you voluntarily do again?

Not Are you good at it?
Did you want to get better?

Those tiny gradients can reconstruct preference without demanding that somebody already possess a fully formed sober identity.

And I think you’re also right to question “age appropriate.” There is a culturally arbitrary component. A 38-year-old assembling LEGO, playing Pokémon, collecting fossils, building model aircraft, climbing trees with his kids, or reading fantasy isn’t inherently developmentally immature.

Adult maturity is better measured by how the interest fits into the whole person.

A man can play Pokémon and also keep promises, raise children, exercise judgment, earn competence, sustain relationships, tolerate frustration, provide for others, and shoulder responsibility. Conversely, somebody can have extremely “adult” hobbies—whiskey, golf, expensive cars—and remain profoundly immature.

So I’d separate childlike from childish.

Childlike: curiosity, play, wonder, experimentation, obsession with learning.

Childish: inability to tolerate limits, deferred gratification, reciprocal obligation, frustration, or responsibility.

Those aren’t the same axis.

And that’s where your “being a man and figuring it out” idea has something useful inside it, provided we don’t define manhood by arbitrary hobbies. Adulthood adds dimensions to desire. You increasingly ask not only What feels good? but:

What am I good at? What am I willing to suffer to become good at? Who needs me? What can I build? What can I protect? What can I teach? What am I curious about? Who do I want beside me? What would I be proud to have spent ten years becoming?

That’s also close to how SAMHSA frames recovery: not merely cessation of substance use, but rebuilding health, home, purpose, and community—with purpose encompassing work, learning, caretaking, volunteering, creative pursuits, hobbies, and meaningful daily activity. 

So I might formalize the thing you’ve noticed as:

Recovery of preference → expansion of identity → accumulation of competence → assumption of responsibility → construction of purpose.

You don’t necessarily discover some pristine adult self that was hiding underneath addiction.

Sometimes you grow one, starting from whatever uncontaminated little signal you can still feel.

And the childhood memory isn’t the destination. It’s the first muscle you can still consciously contract.